A||||7||Sillhouette.com||RSM has replaced their Supercharger with...||||6||
Z||000000||Sillhouette.com||12-15-2000||11:49 AM||||Hello <P>Due to overwhelming requests for Vortech supercharger RSM has finally found a supercharger in the Vortech lineup (G90) that fits the fiero.  It is still the same kit as with the Rotrax supercharger only the supercharger is Vortech.  Like for the Rotrax kit smaller pulleys are available for increased pressure.  Vortech claims that this supercharger puts out 20psi of pressure max.  For the stock 2.8 engine that is not advisable.  12psi is what the original kit works with best.  If you wish to run such high compression lower pressure pistons are advised.  Now for the bad news this kit comes with a higher price.  $4500.00 canadian and $3500.00 US.  Any questions are welcome.   <P>Later||||reg||6
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-15-2000||12:40 PM||||I assume you still lose the air conditioner with this.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wannabe||12-15-2000||01:35 PM||||What was wrong with the Rotrex s/c?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||12-15-2000||01:37 PM||||Isn't RSM also introducing a s/c kit for 3.4L DOHC w-bodies? Will this be adaptable to 3.4L DOHC installs in Fieros?||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||12-15-2000||02:29 PM||||The G trims have been out for 5-6 months now, and I guess now all of a sudden they fit?<P>I seem to remember a month or two ago there was a big discussion about blowers and Fieros...... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sillhouette.com||12-15-2000||04:24 PM||||Yep there was a discussion about three months ago so the G trim would have only been out for two months.  It took Vortech this long to finally get to us and sell us a supercharger of required proportions.  <BR>The AC compressor has to go on the fiero.  The 3.4DOHC supercharger is strictly designed for the Wbodies.  The supercharger will be installed on the front passanger side of the engine instead of the large coolant bottle.  This means that the fiero mounted 3.4 will not be the same.  Not to say that the current supercharger wouldn't fit.  The best would be if someone that has a 3.4DOHC would show up at RSM and have them fit a supercharger kit on that car.  <P>BADD GT<P>Look at it this way the problem with this kit from the begining was that they did not use the "mass" approved supercharger.  That no longer is the case.  If you want rotrax you get rotrax if you want VORTECH you get vortech.  Just more options ..<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000006||BBBAD GT||12-15-2000||06:05 PM||||Couple quick questions:<P>*If you are selling the Vortech unit now, how does it perform compared to the Rotex?  I assume you guys have tested it before selling.<P>*On the Rotex instructions on RSM's web page, here is a quote under "Technological Overview of the ROTREX":<P><B>"The traction gear technology also enables the supercharger to variably adjust the power transfer as this increases with the increased load on the compressor unit. This is a key-feature to ensure the overall break-through system efficiency and performance." </B><P>Can you explain what this means and how different is this lingo from a regular centrifugal supercharger?<P>Just curious.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sillhouette.com||12-15-2000||06:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>Couple quick questions:<P>*If you are selling the Vortech unit now, how does it perform compared to the Rotex?  I assume you guys have tested it before selling.<P>*On the Rotex instructions on RSM's web page, here is a quote under "Technological Overview of the ROTREX":<P>[b]"The traction gear technology also enables the supercharger to variably adjust the power transfer as this increases with the increased load on the compressor unit. This is a key-feature to ensure the overall break-through system efficiency and performance." </B><P>Can you explain what this means and how different is this lingo from a regular centrifugal supercharger?<P>Just curious.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There certainly are differences between the two designs.  The Rotrax has three planetary bearings that rotate CW around a shaft and arount these three bearings there is the casing that is spun by the pulley.  That quotation means that as pressure through the supercharger impellers increases so does the torque required to run the supercharger.  With the Rotrax unit that torque increase is negligable.  With the Vortech unit all you have is a set of gears that are directly connected to each other like a transmission so the torque increase as pressure increases is directly transferred to your crank.  That is no big deal since the pressure output of the Vortech can be changed.  The most you can get with a Vortech is 20psi while the Rotrax can only give you max 12psi.  We have not had the car dynoed yet but there is little difference between the two as far as drivability.  You still get the same acceleration as before. The only difference is that the Boost gauge now shows 12psi while before it only showed 10psi.  The dyno shops are closed for the holiday season so the earliest time that we can get into it will be January.  Than I'll post the dyno chart so that you can see it for yourself.  Good and bad of the two systems.  <BR>Bad for the Rotrax is that it is only able to put out 10psi. It's built in Holland.<BR>Good for the rotrax is that it's light and small.<BR>Bad for the Vortech it's big and very heavy<BR>Good for the Vortech it can put out much more than the Rotrax 20psi.  It's built in USA. <P>Cheers||||reg||1
